-- title:
--   Contracting the terminal blocks of a pinned Ising model is restriction to block-constant spins
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a finite graph on the vertex set $V=\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ with separately indexed edges $e$ (parallel edges allowed), endpoints $\ell(e)\neq r(e)$ and couplings $K_e\ge0$, and let $h\in\mathbb{R}^V$ be an arbitrary external field. A spin configuration $\sigma\in\{-1,+1\}^V$ has weight $w_h(\sigma)=\exp\big(\sum_eK_e\sigma_{\ell(e)}\sigma_{r(e)}+\sum_{v\in V}h_v\sigma_v\big)$.
--
--   Let $I,J,F\subseteq V$ with $I\cap F=\emptyset$ and $J\cap F=\emptyset$, and fix $\tau\in\{-1,+1\}^V$, whose values on $F$ are the pinned spins. For a configuration $\sigma\in\{-1,+1\}^{V\setminus F}$ of the free vertices let $\sigma^{\tau}\in\{-1,+1\}^V$ be the configuration equal to $\tau$ on $F$ and to $\sigma$ on $V\setminus F$. The *contracted pinned weight* keeps only free configurations that are constant on each terminal block:
--   $$
--   W_\tau(\sigma)=\begin{cases}w_h(\sigma^{\tau}) & \text{if }\sigma\text{ is constant on }I\text{ and constant on }J,\\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
--   $$
--   For $a,b\in\{-1,+1\}^V$ let $Z_\tau(a,b)$ be the sum of $w_h(\rho)$ over all $\rho\in\{-1,+1\}^V$ with $\rho=a$ on $I$, $\rho=b$ on $J$ and $\rho=\tau$ on $F$.
--
--   **Theorem.** For all vertices $i\in I$, $j\in J$ and all $s,t\in\{-1,+1\}$,
--   $$
--   \sum_{\sigma\in\{-1,+1\}^{V\setminus F}}\mathbf 1[\sigma_i=s,\ \sigma_j=t]\;W_\tau(\sigma)\;=\;Z_\tau(\underline{s},\underline{t}),
--   $$
--   where $\underline{s}$ and $\underline{t}$ denote the constant configurations with all spins equal to $s$, respectively $t$.
--
--   The left-hand side is the $(s,t)$ entry of the two-site table at $(i,j)$ of the model in which each terminal block is collapsed to a single spin; the right-hand side is the weight of the original pinned model with constant prescriptions on the two blocks. The identity says that identifying each terminal set separately is *exactly* restriction to block-constant configurations, so no comparison between different models enters at this point. It lets the extreme joint weights $Z_\tau(\underline{\pm},\underline{\pm})$ be read off as the entries of a $2\times2$ table, to which effective-coupling arguments apply.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Spins are Booleans (`true` is $+1$, `false` is $-1$). Free configurations are functions `KeptVertex F → Bool`, $\sigma^\tau$ is `pinExtension F τ σ`, and block constancy is `Respects (comparisonSetoid G I J F ∅) σ`, compatibility with the equivalence relation on free vertices generated by "both in $I$" or "both in $J$" (no open edges). The left side is `pairTable (contractedPinnedWeight G h I J F τ) i j s t` and the right side is `jointWeight G h I J F τ (fun _ => s) (fun _ => t)`. The blocks $I$ and $J$ need not be disjoint here.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Probability/Ising/PinnedMarginal.lean#L65-L96

